  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
